. *********************************
. *********************************
. 
.          *HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 
. 
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         *  We assume that gender-specific causal effects of having a daughter
>  are identified wihin strata defined by gender [female], cohort [birthyear] a
> nd education [educ] 
.         * in a sample of parents of a single child (see PAP)
.         * !! NOTE THIS IS THE AVERAGE CAUSAL EFFECT IN THE POPULATION OF PARE
> NTS OF A SINGLE CHILD !!
.         * We further assume that agreement to statements "Women should rather
>  care about family than career" [plh0301] and "Men should involve in housewor
> k" [plh0303] 
.         * are good measures of support for gender equality (hypothesis 1): 
.         * Less agreement to the former and more agreement to the latter is mo
> re likely to indicate support for gender equality.
.         * Accordingly, we run a seemingly unrelated ordered probit regression
>  analysis with famvscareer [plh0301] and menhousework [plh0303] as dependent 
> variables
.         * and the dummy variable daughter as independent variable in a sample
>  of respondents with a single child (parity2!=1), adjusted for covariates
.         * We apply weights cross-sectionally calibrated weights (phrf)
.         * To account for non-independence of observatons, we cluster standard
>  errors at the household level
.         * The null we test against is that the effect of having daughters for
>  men is <= the effect of having daughters for women on both outcomes
.         * I.e., we have to consider a one-sided test adjusted for multiple te
> sting
.         * Since the used method provides a joint confidence band, this is equ
> al to assessing whether there is at least 1 significant effect
.         * We report all coefficients and statistics but only export the resul
> t with the smallest p-value to comply to the provided template
.         * !! NOTE THAT AT AN A PRIORI CONFIDENCE LEVEL OF 0.05 WITHOUT PREDER
> MINED SIGN OF THE EFFECT, THE CONFIDENCE LEVEL BECOMES 0.025
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * The below code follows the conception outlined in the PAP
.         * There are two deviations, one of which reflects perceived risk (2) 
> already anticipated in the PAP:
.         * as full stratification was not possible, we had to impose some cons
> traints.
.         * We decided to only include all pairwise interactions (in addition t
> o all main effects) in the model and omit higher order interactions        
.         * We refrained from more refined methods to determine the functional 
> form on a 5% subsample.
.         * We considered switching to a matching or IPW-weighting based approa
> ch but abandonded the idea in compliance with out PAP - but note that our app
> roach would
.         * call for sensitivity analysis w.r.t. to functional form assumptions
>  when applied to the full estimation sample.
.         * Furthermore, the preliminary code in the PAP mistakenly did not inc
> lude the contrasting of effects by gender.
.         * The code provided below rectifies this error and is in line with th
> e conceptual part of the PAP which targets the gender difference ("delta" in 
> the PAP) rather
.         * then the gender-specific effects themselves ("beta" in the PAP)
.         * Note that the sample restriction is already implemented in the data
>  preparation and therefore omitted for redundancy
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
. 
.         *obtain point esttimates for daughter effects on both outcomes
.         oprobit famvscareer i.daughters i.female i.educ c.birthyear daughters
> #female daughters#educ daughters#c.birthyear female#educ female#c.birthyear e
> duc#c.birthyear [iw=phrf]

